<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shudra">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shudra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shudra is the fourth of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, the classes of traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society described in Vedic and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts, below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> and Vaishyas. Shudras were assigned the duty of serving the other three classes and were excluded from Vedic study and ritual. Several ancient dynasties, including the Nandas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, were said in hostile sources to be of Shudra origin.</p></div>
